{"type":"after","callId":"call@2441","endTime":477848.038,"result":{"value":"FacultyLens\n\nOVERVIEW\n\nDashboard\nAnalytics\nReports\n\nTEACHING\n\nCourses\nAssessments\nQuestion Bank\nHistory\n\nAI TOOLS\n\nAnalysis\nAI\nQuestion Generator\nAI\nDocument Chat\nAI\nAI Evaluation\nAI\n\nWORKSPACE\n\nCollaboration\nNotifications\nFeedback\nSettings\nD\nDr. E2E Faculty\nAssistant Professor\nSite\nSign out\nAssessments\n3\n3 unread notifications\nD\nDr. E2E Faculty\nCSE-902046\n•\nArtificial Intelligence\nAudit Complete\nMidterm Examination\nMidterm Assessment\n•\n30\nMarks\n•\n3\nQuestions\n•\n90 Mins\n•\nAnalyzed Oct 6, 2026, 02:42 AM\nAssessment Report\nView Assessment\nRun AI Analysis\n54\n/ 100\nOVERALL QUALITY SCORE\nREQUIRES ATTENTION\nREQUIRES ATTENTION Assessment Rigor\n\nSignificant imbalance detected across key syllabus modules or evaluation domains.\n\nAcademic Decision Support\n\nInitial assessment-quality indicator based on FacultyLens analysis of six academic dimensions across 3 questions.\n\nHow this score is calculated\nTopic Coverage\n100%\n\nSyllabus modules and key concept representation in assessment items.\n\nLO Coverage\n33%\n\nDirect and semantic alignment to defined Course Learning Outcomes.\n\nDifficulty Balance\n80%\n\nQuestion distribution across Easy, Medium, and Hard tiers vs. target profile.\n\nCognitive Diversity\n0%\n\nSpread across Bloom's levels from foundational to higher-order critique.\n\nQuestion Diversity\n0%\n\nVariety of evaluation formats: conceptual, descriptive, and problem-solving.\n\nMarks Distribution\n100%\n\nAllocation parity ensuring no single problem dominates the total examination score.\n\nDifficulty Distribution\n\nDistribution compared with configured FacultyLens target\n\nBalance: 80%\nEasy\nTarget: 30%\n33%\n(1 Q)\nMedium\nTarget: 50%\n67%\n(2 Qs)\nHard\nTarget: 20%\n0%\n(0 Qs)\nConfigured initial target benchmark (30% Easy, 50% Medium, 20% Hard) serves as guidance. Ideal difficulty balance depends on course objectives and faculty assessment design.\nCognitive-Level Distribution\n\nBloom's Revised Taxonomy classification\n\nDominant: Understand (100%)\nRemember\n0%\n(0 Qs)\nUnderstand\n100%\n(3 Qs)\nApply\n0%\n(0 Qs)\nAnalyze\n0%\n(0 Qs)\nEvaluate\n0%\n(0 Qs)\nCreate\n0%\n(0 Qs)\nCognitive distribution should be interpreted in relation to the course learning outcomes and assessment purpose rather than assuming higher Bloom tiers are universally preferable.\nTopic Coverage\n\nIdentified course topics evaluated in questions\n\nCoverage\n100%\nSearch algorithms\n3 questions • 30 marks\n100%\nCovered\nLearning Outcome Alignment\n\nSemantic mapping to course learning outcomes\n\nOverall Alignment\n33%\nLO1\n3 questions\n\nExplain uninformed and informed search strategies.\n\nSemantic similarity: 0.60\nWeak\nLO2\n0 questions\n\nApply knowledge representation techniques to model a domain.\n\nSemantic similarity: 0.00\nNot Aligned\nLO3\n0 questions\n\nEvaluate machine learning models using appropriate metrics.\n\nSemantic similarity: 0.00\nNot Aligned\nQuestion → Learning Outcome Mapping (3)\nSimilar Questions\n\nSemantic overlap detected against historical question bank\n\nPotential Duplicates: 0\nHighly Similar: 0\nSomewhat: 0\nNo significant semantic similarity detected with past examination questions. All current questions appear original.\nSemantic similarity is evidence for faculty review, not proof of exact duplication. Verify question contexts before deciding whether revisions are needed.\nAI Findings\n\nSynthesis of assessment strengths and potential risks\n\n1\n\nTopic Coverage: All defined syllabus topics are represented across the examination.\n\n2\n\nLO Coverage: 2 Learning Outcome(s) have no mapped questions (LO2, LO3).\n\n3\n\nCognitive Diversity: High concentration detected — 100.0% of marks are at the 'Understand' level. Faculty review recommended.\n\n4\n\nCognitive Diversity: No higher-order cognitive questions (0% Analyze, Evaluate, Create).\n\n5\n\nQuestion Diversity: Single format detected — 100% of questions are 'Descriptive'. Consider diversifying question types if appropriate for the assessment format.\n\n6\n\nUnassessed Learning Outcomes: LO2, LO3 currently have no matching exam questions (similarity < 0.5).\n\n7\n\nMarginal Coverage: LO1 are only weakly represented by assessment questions.\n\n8\n\nImbalanced Assessment: 100% of questions map to LO1, indicating potential over-concentration.\n\nAI Recommendations\n\nActionable, evidence-based recommendations generated for faculty decision\n\n6 of 6\nPriority:\nAll Priorities\nHigh\nMedium\nLow\nStatus:\nAll Statuses\nPending\nReviewed\nAccepted\nDismissed\nCategory:\nAll Categories\nlearning outcome\nquestion diversity\ncognitive level\ndifficulty\nHIGH PRIORITY\nLearning Outcome\n• Learning Outcome Alignment\nWhy?\nPending\nLO3 has no aligned examination questions\n\nWhy this matters: None of the examination questions demonstrate sufficient semantic alignment with LO3.\n\nRecommendation:\n\nConsider designing or updating an assessment question to specifically target LO3. Align the question phrasing and tasks with the competency verbs defined in the LO.\n\nEvidence Details\nFaculty decision support — decisions do not automatically alter assessment questions.\nReview\nAccept\nDismiss\nHIGH PRIORITY\nLearning Outcome\n• Learning Outcome Alignment\nWhy?\nPending\nLO2 has no aligned examination questions\n\nWhy this matters: None of the examination questions demonstrate sufficient semantic alignment with LO2.\n\nRecommendation:\n\nConsider designing or updating an assessment question to specifically target LO2. Align the question phrasing and tasks with the competency verbs defined in the LO.\n\nEvidence Details\nFaculty decision support — decisions do not automatically alter assessment questions.\nReview\nAccept\nDismiss\nMEDIUM\nQuestion Diversity\n• Question Format Diversity\nWhy?\nPending\nAll questions are single format ('Descriptive')\n\nWhy this matters: The entire examination utilizes only 'Descriptive' questions without format diversity.\n\nRecommendation:\n\nThe examination relies entirely on 'Descriptive' items. Consider blending multiple formats (e.g., Problem Solving, Conceptual Design, Structured Scenarios) to assess diverse student skills.\n\nEvidence Details\nFaculty decision support — decisions do not automatically alter assessment questions.\nReview\nAccept\nDismiss\nMEDIUM\nCognitive Level\n• Bloom Cognitive Distribution\nWhy?\nPending\nHeavy reliance on Lower-Order cognitive tasks (100.0%)\n\nWhy this matters: Remember and Understand level questions constitute 100.0% of the evaluation.\n\nRecommendation:\n\nLower-order cognitive levels (Remember/Understand) constitute 100.0% of total evaluation. Consider upgrading selected questions to Apply, Analyze, or Design tasks.\n\nEvidence Details\nFaculty decision support — decisions do not automatically alter assessment questions.\nReview\nAccept\nDismiss\nMEDIUM\nCognitive Level\n• Bloom Cognitive Distribution\nWhy?\nPending\nCognitive concentration at 'Understand' level (100.0%)\n\nWhy this matters: A large proportion of questions/marks (100.0%) is concentrated at the 'Understand' Bloom taxonomy level.\n\nRecommendation:\n\nWith 100.0% of evaluation concentrated in 'Understand', consider incorporating questions from higher cognitive tiers (e.g., Analyze, Evaluate, Create) to stimulate deeper student inquiry.\n\nEvidence Details\nFaculty decision support — decisions do not automatically alter assessment questions.\nReview\nAccept\nDismiss\nMEDIUM\nDifficulty\n• Difficulty Balance Analysis\nWhy?\nPending\nNo advanced (Hard) difficulty questions found\n\nWhy this matters: The assessment lacks rigorous challenge questions to distinguish high-performing students.\n\nRecommendation:\n\nConsider incorporating at least one advanced, multi-step problem-solving or synthesis question to appropriately challenge and distinguish top-tier mastery.\n\nEvidence Details\nFaculty decision